---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Colophon · San Lee"
  description="How this site was built. I set the direction, the contracts, and the bar; Claude did most of the typing; the evals and postmortems are the proof."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/colophon.html"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* How-it-was-made plate. I set, it types, I check. Open pipeline,
       no oxide: this is the method, not a reversal. */
    .made-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .made-plate .md-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .made-plate .md-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .made-plate .md-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .made-plate .md-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .made-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .made-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 18px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Colophon</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How this site was built. The method, and the session record that backs it.
    </p>
    <svg class="made-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="How this site was built: I set the direction, an agent did most of the typing, I verified the output against the real repos before it shipped.">
      <title>I set the aim. The agent types. I check the output.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="md-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <rect class="md-box" x="2" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="40" y="24" text-anchor="middle">I set</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="40" y="38" text-anchor="middle">direction</text>
      <path class="md-edge" d="M 78 26 L 90 26" marker-end="url(#md-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="md-box" x="92" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">It types</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">the agent</text>
      <path class="md-edge" d="M 168 26 L 180 26" marker-end="url(#md-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="md-box" x="182" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="md-title" x="220" y="24" text-anchor="middle">I check</text>
      <text class="md-micro" x="220" y="38" text-anchor="middle">then it ships</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="made-plate-caption">
      I set the direction. Claude typed. The ledger is the proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site was built the way I would run any small project with an agent: I
      set the direction, the contracts, and the bar; Claude did most of the
      typing; and I verified the output against the real repos before it
      shipped. The evals and postmortems are the proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      That claim gets the same standard as everything else here: evidence over
      assertion. Some of those calls were mine, some weren&rsquo;t. The ledger
      below is the record of which.
    </p>

    <h2>The ledger</h2>
    <p>
      This is a sample from the actual session transcripts, spanning the whole
      build. Dates are real. Quotes are tightened to the site&rsquo;s writing
      standard. I didn&rsquo;t
      filter out the entries that make either of us look bad &mdash; and since
      early July the worst of them come with public receipts: the operating
      layer now keeps its
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops">incident log in the open</a>,
      postmortem-first. The early-July quotes are lifted straight from those
      postmortems and the linked records; the later ones come from the
      session transcripts themselves. The same scrutiny turned on my own
      instruments rather than on the agent is
      <a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green</a>: six checks that
      reported success for work that had not happened.
    </p>
    <!--
      Curation bar for this ledger (read before editing):
      Every entry must show human judgment changing the outcome — a catch, a
      refusal, a redirect, or the agent's own case winning on merit. If an entry
      reads as "Claude did a task well," it does not belong here; the page's
      claim is that the *direction* was the work, so the quote should almost
      always be San's, not the agent being clever.
      This is a curated sample, not a growing log. Keep it to ~6-8, and as
      stronger steering moments land, SWAP one in — do not append.
    -->
    <ol class="timeline">
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 21</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Sometimes its case won.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">It pushed back on my docs format, Markdown over RST; I weighed the reasoning and took it. Steering cuts both ways &mdash; the point isn&rsquo;t that I&rsquo;m always right, it&rsquo;s that someone is deciding. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;If you decide the format is more than we need, keep that decision.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jun 27</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It insisted the system still used Kafka.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">The local clone it trusted was out of date, so it argued a fact that had changed. That became a standing rule: verify before asserting. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;The system does not use Kafka now. Confirm this.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 2</div>
        <div class="tl-title">It burned a whole usage window.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">A scoped question set off an uncapped fan-out of thirty-plus agents on the most expensive model tier; I caught it mid-flight and killed it, an entire five-hour window gone in forty-five minutes. The fix is a hook that mechanically blocks any uncapped run (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-02-uncapped-premium-fanout.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Spend was authorized with no review, by default, and with no gate.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 3</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I asked for holes; it found one in its own guard.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">On an open audit request it bypassed its own credential guard &mdash; the hook built after real leaks &mdash; with an interpreter one-liner, proving the gap safely before a live one could. The prompt that mattered was mine; the finding was real (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/agent-ops/blob/main/incidents/2026-07-03-credential-guard-interpreter-bypass.md">postmortem</a>). <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Tell me every gap, leak, vulnerability, and risk.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 26</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I retired a framing it kept building on.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">Its operating notes had this site filed as my &ldquo;front-end learning vehicle,&rdquo; and work kept getting scoped around that instead of around the portfolio. I killed the framing mid-redesign and had the record rewritten so it stayed dead. Part of directing is deciding what a project <em>is</em>. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;I do not have a &lsquo;front end learning vehicle&rsquo;.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Jul 28</div>
        <div class="tl-title">Its whole pipeline approved a design. I didn&rsquo;t.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">A redesign had survived five direction prototypes, a judging panel, and an adversarial review &mdash; and still looked wrong on my screen. Instead of arguing taste, I asked for variants: a three-way screenshot A/B settled it, and the serif came out <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/pull/182">the same day</a>. Process blesses; it doesn&rsquo;t decide. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;I do not know if I accept this font. Explain the night-light background.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tl-date">Aug 2</div>
        <div class="tl-title">I made it read this site cold.</div>
        <p class="tl-body">I had it review the site as a hiring manager who knows nothing about me &mdash; no context, no credit for effort. The verdict was blunt and correct: the homepage explained how I work before it said who I am. The positioning was <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/pull/200">re-cut the same day</a>. <span class="tl-quote">&ldquo;Assume you know nothing about me.&rdquo;</span></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
